const DATA={"reading":{"1":[{"t":"The Busy Bee","p":"A little bee flies from flower to flower. It lands on a yellow flower and drinks sweet nectar. Then it carries pollen to the next flower. The bee works until the sun begins to go down.","learn":"Good readers look for who, what happened and where it happened.","qs":[["What does the bee drink?",["Nectar","Milk","Rain","Juice"],0,"Find the detail in the second sentence."],["What does the bee carry?",["Pollen","Sand","Leaves","Stones"],0,"Look for what the bee takes to the next flower."],["What is the main idea?",["A bee works among flowers","A bird builds a nest","A child plants a tree","The sun is hot"],0,"Think about what most sentences are about."]]},{"t":"Mia’s Lunchbox","p":"Mia opens her lunchbox at school. She has a cheese sandwich, a red apple and a bottle of water. She eats the apple first. Her friend Ava sits beside her. They talk quietly while they eat.","learn":"Use small details in the passage to answer questions.","qs":[["What fruit does Mia have?",["An apple","A banana","An orange","A pear"],0,"The fruit is described by colour."],["Who sits beside Mia?",["Ava","Mum","Sam","Dad"],0,"Look at the fourth sentence."],["Where is Mia?",["At school","At the beach","At home","At the zoo"],0,"The first sentence tells you."]]},{"t":"The Windy Day","p":"The wind is strong today. Leo puts on his jacket and holds his hat. Leaves race along the footpath. A kite flies high above the park. Leo laughs when the wind pushes him gently from behind.","learn":"A passage can tell you what happens and how a character feels.","qs":[["Why does Leo hold his hat?",["The wind is strong","It is raining","The hat is heavy","He is indoors"],0,"Connect the first two sentences."],["What flies above the park?",["A kite","A plane","A leaf","A ball"],0,"Find the object that flies high."],["How does Leo feel?",["Happy","Angry","Scared","Sleepy"],0,"The word 'laughs' is a clue."]]},{"t":"Grandma’s Cake","p":"Grandma bakes a small chocolate cake. Noor helps mix the batter and puts paper cases on the tray. The kitchen smells sweet. When the cake is cool, Grandma cuts two pieces. Noor says it tastes delicious.","learn":"Sequence words help you understand what happened first and next.","qs":[["What flavour is the cake?",["Chocolate","Lemon","Vanilla","Apple"],0,"Look in the first sentence."],["What happens before Grandma cuts the cake?",["The cake cools","Noor goes home","They wash the car","The cake is eaten"],0,"Look for the event just before cutting."],["Which word tells how the cake tastes?",["Delicious","Small","Cool","Paper"],0,"Find the describing word about taste."]]}],"2":[{"t":"The Missing Library Book","p":"Ethan was ready to return his library books, but one book was missing. He checked his school bag, his desk and the shelf beside his bed. At last, he noticed a corner of the book under a cushion on the sofa. Ethan smiled, packed it carefully and left for school.","learn":"When reading, follow the problem and the way it is solved.","qs":[["What problem does Ethan have?",["A library book is missing","His bag is broken","He misses the bus","He cannot read"],0,"Look at the first sentence."],["Where does he find the book?",["Under a sofa cushion","In his desk","At school","On a shelf"],0,"Find the final place he checks."],["Why does Ethan smile?",["He found the book","He bought a new book","School is closed","He found money"],0,"Think about how the problem was solved."],["What happens last?",["He leaves for school","He checks his desk","He looks on the shelf","He opens his bag"],0,"Use the order of events."]]},{"t":"Planting Sunflowers","p":"Our class planted sunflower seeds in small pots. We filled each pot with soil, pushed one seed into the middle and added water. Every day, two students checked the pots. After a week, tiny green shoots appeared. We moved the pots near the sunny window so the plants could keep growing.","learn":"Procedural texts often describe steps in order.","qs":[["What did the class put in the pots first?",["Soil","Flowers","Paint","Sand"],0,"Find the first step."],["What appeared after a week?",["Green shoots","Yellow flowers","Fruit","Roots above the soil"],0,"Look for the time clue 'after a week'."],["Why were the pots moved?",["To get sunlight","To hide them","To cool them","To empty them"],0,"The last sentence explains why."],["What is the passage mostly about?",["Growing sunflowers","Cleaning windows","Buying pots","Learning to paint"],0,"Choose the idea that covers the whole passage."]]},{"t":"The Helpful Neighbour","p":"Mrs Chen carried two heavy shopping bags toward her gate. Aria saw her from across the street and asked if she needed help. Aria carried one bag to the kitchen while Mrs Chen carried the other. Mrs Chen thanked her and gave her a bright smile. Aria felt proud that a small act had made someone’s day easier.","learn":"Characters' actions can show kindness, responsibility and feelings.","qs":[["Why does Aria offer to help?",["The bags are heavy","She wants a toy","It is raining","She is late"],0,"Connect the first two sentences."],["How does Mrs Chen respond?",["She thanks Aria","She becomes angry","She leaves the bags","She closes the gate"],0,"Look at the fourth sentence."],["How does Aria feel at the end?",["Proud","Bored","Hungry","Afraid"],0,"The final sentence states the feeling."],["What lesson fits the passage?",["Small acts of help matter","Shopping is difficult","Never cross a street","Kitchens are busy"],0,"Think beyond one detail to the message."]]},{"t":"A Night at Camp","p":"The Year 2 campers sat in a circle outside their cabins. Their teacher showed them how to toast bread safely over the small fire. Above them, the sky grew dark and stars appeared one by one. Later, everyone used a torch to walk back to the cabins. Maya whispered that it had been her favourite part of camp.","learn":"Setting tells when and where a story happens.","qs":[["Where are the children?",["At camp","At a zoo","At home","At a shop"],0,"Look for cabins, fire and campers."],["What appears in the sky?",["Stars","Rainbows","Kites","Balloons"],0,"Read the third sentence."],["Why do they use torches?",["It is dark","They are reading","They are cooking","They lost the fire"],0,"Use the setting clue."],["Who says it was her favourite part?",["Maya","The teacher","Aria","Mrs Chen"],0,"Look at the last sentence."]]}],"3":[{"t":"The Creek Cleanup","p":"On Saturday morning, a group of families met beside Willow Creek. They wore gloves and carried reusable bags. Some people collected plastic bottles while others picked up wrappers and old fishing line. By lunchtime, the banks looked cleaner and several bags were full. The group placed recyclable items in separate bins and promised to return next month.","learn":"Readers identify the main idea and supporting details.","qs":[["Why did the group meet?",["To clean the creek","To go swimming","To catch fish","To build a bridge"],0,"Think about what all the actions have in common."],["Which item was collected?",["Plastic bottles","School books","Fresh fruit","Wooden toys"],0,"Find one listed piece of rubbish."],["What did they do with recyclable items?",["Put them in separate bins","Burned them","Left them beside the creek","Took them fishing"],0,"Read the last sentence carefully."],["What can you infer about the group?",["They care about the environment","They dislike the creek","They work at a shop","They are afraid of water"],0,"Use their actions as evidence."]]},{"t":"Why Bees Matter","p":"Bees are small insects, but they do an important job. As bees visit flowers to collect nectar, pollen sticks to their bodies. Some of this pollen moves to other flowers. This process helps many plants produce fruit and seeds. Without pollinators such as bees, farmers would find it harder to grow many foods that people eat.","learn":"Informative texts explain facts and often use cause and effect.","qs":[["What sticks to bees?",["Pollen","Soil","Rain","Seeds"],0,"Look at the second sentence."],["What does pollination help plants produce?",["Fruit and seeds","Rocks and water","Leaves only","Honey"],0,"Read the fourth sentence."],["Why are bees important to farmers?",["They help plants produce food","They carry farm tools","They scare birds","They make rain"],0,"Connect the final two sentences."],["What type of text is this?",["Informative","A fairy tale","A recipe","A poem"],0,"It explains facts about a topic."]]},{"t":"The Surprise Goal","p":"The score was tied with one minute left in the game. Liam received the ball near the sideline, but two defenders blocked his path. Instead of trying to run past them, he passed to Zara in the centre. Zara saw an open teammate and passed again. The final shot went straight into the goal. The team cheered because quick teamwork had created the chance.","learn":"A character's decision can change what happens next.","qs":[["Why does Liam pass the ball?",["Two defenders block him","He is tired of playing","The game has ended","He cannot see Zara"],0,"Find the problem he faces."],["What creates the scoring chance?",["Teamwork","A lucky bounce only","The referee","The weather"],0,"The final sentence names it."],["What does 'tied' mean here?",["Both teams have the same score","A rope is knotted","The game is cancelled","One team is winning"],0,"Use the sports context."],["What is the best message?",["Working together can solve problems","Never pass the ball","Only one player matters","Games should be short"],0,"Think about why the goal happened."]]},{"t":"The Old Map","p":"In a box at the back of the cupboard, Sienna found a folded map with faded blue lines. Her grandfather explained that he had used it on bushwalks many years ago. The map did not show new roads or buildings, but it marked creeks, hills and walking tracks. Sienna compared it with a modern map and noticed how much the town had changed.","learn":"Compare-and-contrast questions ask what is the same or different.","qs":[["Who used the old map?",["Sienna’s grandfather","Sienna’s teacher","A shopkeeper","Her brother"],0,"Read the second sentence."],["What was missing from the old map?",["New roads and buildings","Creeks and hills","Walking tracks","Blue lines"],0,"Find the contrast in the third sentence."],["What did Sienna compare?",["Old and modern maps","Two books","Two cupboards","Two towns"],0,"Read the last sentence."],["What did she learn?",["The town had changed","The map was new","There were no creeks","Her grandfather disliked walking"],0,"Use the conclusion of the passage."]]}],"4":[{"t":"A Tiny Forest in the City","p":"A vacant block beside the community centre once contained only dry grass and weeds. Local volunteers decided to turn it into a tiny native forest. They planted many different species close together, covered the soil with mulch and watered the young plants during dry weeks. Within two years, insects and small birds were visiting the site. The trees were still young, but the block had become cooler, greener and more inviting.","learn":"Strong readers connect details to explain how and why change happens.","qs":[["What was the block like at first?",["Dry and weedy","Covered in tall forest","Full of shops","A playground"],0,"Use the first sentence."],["What helped protect the soil?",["Mulch","Concrete","Plastic","Paint"],0,"Find the action after planting."],["What change happened after two years?",["Wildlife began visiting","All trees were removed","The centre closed","The block became hotter"],0,"Read the fourth sentence."],["Which idea best summarises the text?",["Community planting transformed an empty block","Birds are difficult to find","Mulch is expensive","Cities should have no buildings"],0,"Choose the statement covering beginning, action and result."]]},{"t":"The Science Fair Choice","p":"Mina wanted her science fair project to look impressive, so she first planned a complicated robot. After reading the rules, she realised that she needed to explain a clear scientific question and collect evidence. She changed her plan and tested which material kept an ice cube frozen for the longest time. Her display was simple, but her measurements were careful and her conclusion matched the results.","learn":"Authors often show how a character learns or changes.","qs":[["Why does Mina change her project?",["She understands the rules better","The robot wins a prize","She loses her ice cubes","Her teacher tells her to quit"],0,"Look at what she realises."],["What does she test?",["Insulating materials","Robot speed","Plant growth","Water colour"],0,"Read the third sentence."],["What was strong about her final project?",["Careful evidence","A complicated machine","Bright decorations only","No measurements"],0,"Use the last sentence."],["What lesson does Mina learn?",["Clear evidence matters more than complexity","Robots are always better","Simple projects need no planning","Rules should be ignored"],0,"Infer from the change she makes."]]},{"t":"How a Billabong Changes","p":"A billabong can look very different across the year. During wet periods, rain and river flows may fill it with fresh water. Aquatic plants grow quickly, and frogs and waterbirds may arrive. During long dry periods, the water level can fall and some parts may become muddy or dry. Many Australian plants and animals have behaviours or life cycles that help them survive these changing conditions.","learn":"Look for sequence, comparison and cause-and-effect in informative texts.","qs":[["What may fill a billabong?",["Rain and river flows","Ocean tides only","Snowfall","Pipes from houses"],0,"Read the second sentence."],["What can happen in dry periods?",["Water levels fall","Frogs always increase","The billabong becomes an ocean","All plants grow faster"],0,"Look for the contrast."],["Why do some animals survive the changes?",["They have helpful behaviours or life cycles","They leave Australia forever","They do not need water","They build roads"],0,"Use the final sentence."],["How is the text organised?",["By describing changes across wet and dry times","As a recipe","As a dialogue","As a set of jokes"],0,"Think about the structure."]]},{"t":"The Debate Team","p":"At first, Noah thought winning a debate meant speaking the loudest. During practice, his coach asked the team to support every claim with evidence and to listen carefully to the opposing side. Noah began taking notes while others spoke. In the final debate, he used a calm voice, answered a counterargument and referred to facts from his research. Even before the result was announced, he knew he had become a stronger speaker.","learn":"Evidence from actions and dialogue helps readers infer character growth.","qs":[["What did Noah believe at first?",["Loud speaking would win","Evidence was important","Listening was easy","Research was unnecessary"],0,"Read the first sentence."],["What new habit does he develop?",["Taking notes while listening","Shouting more often","Ignoring opponents","Speaking without facts"],0,"Find the change during practice."],["What is a counterargument?",["An opposing point of view","A final score","A loud voice","A research book"],0,"Use the debate context."],["Why does Noah feel successful before the result?",["His skills improved","He knows he won","The other team leaves","The coach gives him a prize"],0,"Infer from the last sentence."]]}],"5":[{"t":"The Plastic-Free Lunch Challenge","p":"Our school environmental committee noticed that bins were filling with single-use wrappers after lunch. Rather than banning packaged food immediately, the committee ran a two-week challenge. Classes measured how many pieces of lunch rubbish they produced each day, then students tried reusable containers, refillable bottles and cloth snack bags. By the second week, the amount of rubbish had fallen sharply. The committee concluded that measuring the problem first helped students see that small choices could make a visible difference.","learn":"Year 5 readers identify claims, evidence and conclusions.","qs":[["What problem did the committee notice?",["Too much lunch packaging waste","Not enough lunch time","Broken bottles","No school bins"],0,"Find the issue in the first sentence."],["Why did classes measure rubbish?",["To track whether choices made a difference","To make lunch longer","To choose a winner only","To count students"],0,"Connect the measurement to the conclusion."],["Which evidence supports the committee’s conclusion?",["Rubbish fell sharply by week two","Students liked lunch","The committee met","Bins were colourful"],0,"Choose the measured result."],["What is the author's main message?",["Small reusable choices can reduce waste","All packaged food should be banned","Measuring is unnecessary","Cloth bags are expensive"],0,"Use the whole passage, especially the conclusion."]]},{"t":"A Different Route","p":"The hiking group planned to follow a ridge track to the lookout, but dark clouds gathered faster than expected. Their leader checked the weather radar and noticed a storm approaching from the west. Although the shorter ridge route was familiar, it offered little shelter. The group chose a longer forest track that stayed at a lower elevation. They reached the car park later than planned, but safely, just as heavy rain began.","learn":"Inference questions ask you to combine clues rather than copy one sentence.","qs":[["Why is the ridge route risky?",["It has little shelter during the storm","It is too short","It is below sea level","It is closed by snow"],0,"Combine the storm clue and shelter clue."],["Why choose the longer route?",["It is safer","It is faster","It has no trees","It reaches another town"],0,"Infer from elevation and shelter."],["What does the ending show?",["The group's decision was sensible","The storm disappeared","The radar was wrong","They became lost"],0,"Use the timing of the heavy rain."],["Which trait best describes the leader?",["Cautious","Careless","Impatient","Forgetful"],0,"Judge the leader's decisions."]]},{"t":"Why Mangroves Protect Coasts","p":"Mangrove forests grow along sheltered tropical and subtropical coasts. Their tangled roots slow moving water and can trap sediment. During some storms, this dense vegetation can reduce the energy of waves before they reach land. Mangroves also provide habitat for fish, crabs and birds, and they store carbon in their wood and soils. Protecting mangroves therefore supports both coastal communities and ecosystems, although they are only one part of broader coastal protection.","learn":"Reliable summaries include the central idea and important qualifications.","qs":[["How can mangrove roots affect water?",["They slow it and trap sediment","They heat it","They turn it fresh","They remove all waves"],0,"Read the second sentence."],["Which is another mangrove benefit?",["Wildlife habitat","Making concrete","Increasing wave energy","Removing all storms"],0,"Find a benefit beyond coastal protection."],["Why does the writer say 'only one part'?",["Mangroves cannot solve every coastal risk alone","Mangroves have no value","Only engineers protect coasts","Mangroves grow everywhere"],0,"Notice the qualification in the final sentence."],["Best summary?",["Mangroves provide several environmental and coastal benefits","Mangroves are only useful to fish","All coasts have mangroves","Storms cannot cross mangroves"],0,"Include more than one major benefit."]]},{"t":"The Group Project Problem","p":"Four students were preparing a history presentation, but their work was uneven. Tahlia had completed most of the research while two members had focused mainly on choosing images. Instead of finishing everything herself, Tahlia suggested dividing the remaining tasks and setting a short deadline for each one. She also created a shared checklist so everyone could see what was finished. The presentation improved, but more importantly, the group understood how clearer roles could make teamwork fairer.","learn":"Theme is often revealed through a problem, response and outcome.","qs":[["What makes the work uneven?",["One student has done most research","The presentation has no images","The deadline is too long","History is too difficult"],0,"Compare what each member has done."],["What solution does Tahlia suggest?",["Clear tasks and deadlines","Doing everything herself","Removing all images","Cancelling the presentation"],0,"Read the third sentence."],["Why is the checklist useful?",["Everyone can see progress","It adds more research","It chooses the topic","It changes the deadline"],0,"The fourth sentence explains its purpose."],["What theme fits best?",["Clear responsibilities improve teamwork","Group work should be avoided","Images matter most","Fast work is always best"],0,"Use the final sentence."]]}],"6":[{"t":"The Value of Urban Trees","p":"Urban trees are sometimes discussed mainly as decoration, yet their effects can be practical as well as visual. Tree canopies shade streets and buildings, which may reduce surface temperatures on hot days. Leaves can intercept some rainfall, while roots and soil can slow the movement of water into drains. Trees also provide habitat and can make walking routes more comfortable. However, species choice, maintenance, available soil and water all influence whether these benefits are achieved over time.","learn":"Year 6 readers distinguish evidence, claims and qualifications in complex texts.","qs":[["Which benefit relates directly to heat?",["Shade can reduce surface temperatures","Roots make streets wider","Leaves remove all pollution","Trees stop every storm"],0,"Find the sentence about hot days."],["Why does the author include 'however'?",["To qualify the benefits with conditions","To prove trees are harmful","To change to an unrelated topic","To repeat the first sentence"],0,"Look at how the final sentence changes the claim."],["What does 'intercept' most nearly mean?",["Catch before it reaches the ground","Create","Heat","Measure"],0,"Use the rainfall context."],["Which summary is most accurate?",["Urban trees can provide several benefits, but outcomes depend on planning and care","Urban trees are only decorative","All tree species give identical benefits","Cities should replace drains with trees"],0,"Include both benefits and the qualification."]]},{"t":"The Forgotten Experiment","p":"During a classroom experiment, students placed identical plants in three locations to investigate how light affected growth. After several weeks, one group announced that the plant beside the window had grown tallest. Maya noticed, however, that this plant had also been watered more often because it was close to the sink. She argued that the class could not confidently attribute the extra growth to light alone. The class repeated the experiment using the same watering schedule for every plant.","learn":"Critical readers evaluate whether evidence really supports a conclusion.","qs":[["What is the investigation meant to test?",["Effect of light on growth","Effect of soil colour","Effect of pot size","Effect of music"],0,"Read the first sentence."],["What extra factor changes the result?",["Watering frequency","Plant type","Pot size","Length of the experiment"],0,"Find Maya's observation."],["Why repeat the experiment?",["To control the watering variable","To make plants shorter","To change the question","To avoid measuring"],0,"Connect the flaw to the new design."],["What skill does Maya demonstrate?",["Critical evaluation of evidence","Guessing","Memorisation only","Creative drawing"],0,"Consider what she notices about the conclusion."]]},{"t":"Should Homework Be Limited?","p":"Some students argue that heavy homework loads reduce time for sleep, exercise and family activities. Others point out that carefully chosen homework can provide useful practice and help students prepare for upcoming lessons. The disagreement is partly about quantity and purpose, not simply whether homework is good or bad. A sensible policy would consider students' ages, the value of each task and the total workload across subjects.","learn":"Balanced argument texts may present more than one viewpoint before reaching a qualified position.","qs":[["What concern is raised about heavy homework?",["It can reduce time for other important activities","It always improves sleep","It removes school lessons","It makes exercise easier"],0,"Look at the first sentence."],["What benefit can suitable homework provide?",["Practice and preparation","Longer school holidays","Less learning","More screen time"],0,"Read the second sentence."],["What is the writer's position?",["Homework should be judged by purpose, age and total workload","All homework should end","More homework is always better","Only one subject should assign work"],0,"Use the final two sentences."],["Why is the passage balanced?",["It presents benefits and concerns","It uses no evidence","It only argues one side","It is written as a story"],0,"Notice the two viewpoints."]]},{"t":"The Last Relay Change","p":"With one lap remaining in the relay, the team was running second. Their final runner, Nia, had the fastest sprint time, but the incoming runner looked exhausted. During the changeover, Nia began moving early so their speeds would match. The baton transfer was smooth, allowing her to accelerate without losing momentum. She crossed the finish line first by less than a second. Afterwards, the coach praised not only Nia's speed but also the team's repeated practice of the changeover.","learn":"Authors may contrast an obvious cause with a deeper cause to shape the message.","qs":[["What obvious advantage does Nia have?",["She is the fastest sprinter","She starts first","She carries two batons","She runs a shorter lap"],0,"Read the second sentence."],["Why does Nia begin moving early?",["To match the incoming runner's speed","To leave the race","To slow down","To avoid receiving the baton"],0,"Read the third sentence."],["What does the coach emphasise?",["Practice and teamwork as well as speed","Only the final runner","The colour of the baton","Luck"],0,"Use the last sentence."],["What is the deeper message?",["Preparation and coordination can be decisive","The fastest person always wins alone","Relays need no practice","Second place is failure"],0,"Contrast individual speed with the team skill."]]}]},"grammar":{"1":[{"t":"Capital Letters","learn":"Start every sentence and every special name with a capital letter.","qs":[["Which sentence starts correctly?",["the dog runs.","The dog runs.","the Dog runs.","THE dog runs."],1,"A sentence begins with a capital letter."],["Which word needs a capital letter: 'my friend sam is here'?",["my","friend","sam","here"],2,"Names of people begin with capitals."],["Choose the correct sentence.",["I live in Sydney.","i live in Sydney.","I live in sydney.","i live in sydney."],0,"The sentence and place name both need capitals."],["Which name is written correctly?",["mia","Mia","mIA","MIAa"],1,"A person's name starts with one capital letter."]]},{"t":"Full Stops","learn":"A telling sentence usually ends with a full stop.","qs":[["Which sentence ends correctly?",["I have a red hat","I have a red hat.","I have a red hat?","I have a red hat!?"],1,"A simple statement ends with a full stop."],["What mark belongs at the end? 'The cat is sleeping__'",[".",",","?","'"],0,"This sentence tells something."],["Which is a complete sentence?",["blue car","Runs fast","The blue car is fast.","under the table"],2,"A complete sentence tells a whole idea."],["Choose the best punctuation: 'We walk to school__'",[".",":","?",";"],0,"It is a statement."]]},{"t":"Nouns","learn":"A noun names a person, place, animal or thing.","qs":[["Which word is a noun?",["jump","happy","teacher","quickly"],2,"Teacher names a person."],["Which word names a place?",["school","run","soft","sing"],0,"School is a place."],["Find the noun in 'The rabbit hops.'",["The","rabbit","hops","."],1,"Rabbit names an animal."],["Which pair are both nouns?",["dog, park","run, jump","happy, sad","slowly, softly"],0,"Dog and park name a thing/animal and a place."]]},{"t":"Verbs","learn":"A verb tells what someone or something does.","qs":[["Find the verb: 'Mia jumps high.'",["Mia","jumps","high","."],1,"Jumps is the action."],["Which word is an action?",["run","blue","table","small"],0,"Run tells what someone can do."],["Choose the verb in 'The bird sings.'",["The","bird","sings","."],2,"Sings is the action."],["Which sentence has the verb 'plays'?",["Sam plays ball.","The red ball.","Sam is happy.","A big park."],0,"Plays tells the action."]]},{"t":"Singular and Plural","learn":"Singular means one. Plural means more than one. Many plurals add s.","qs":[["What is the plural of 'cat'?",["cats","cates","cat's","catt"],0,"Add s for this regular plural."],["Which word means more than one?",["dog","dogs","a dog","one dog"],1,"Dogs means more than one dog."],["What is the singular of 'books'?",["book","bookes","books","booking"],0,"Remove the plural s."],["Choose the correct phrase.",["three apples","three apple","three appls","three apple's"],0,"After three, use the plural form."]]}],"2":[{"t":"Sentence Types","learn":"Statements tell, questions ask, commands direct, and exclamations show strong feeling.","qs":[["Which is a question?",["Please sit down.","Where is my bag?","I found my bag.","What a huge bag!"],1,"Questions ask for information and end with ?"],["Which is a command?",["Close the door.","The door is blue.","Is the door open?","What a loud door!"],0,"A command tells someone what to do."],["Which sentence is an exclamation?",["We won the game!","We played the game.","Did we win?","Play the game."],0,"The exclamation shows strong feeling."],["Which punctuation ends most questions?",["?","!",".",","],0,"Questions end with a question mark."]]},{"t":"Adjectives","learn":"Adjectives describe nouns. They can tell colour, size, shape, number or quality.","qs":[["Which word describes the dog in 'The small dog barked'?",["small","dog","barked","the"],0,"Small describes the noun dog."],["Choose the adjective.",["bright","run","chair","quickly"],0,"Bright is a describing word."],["Which phrase has an adjective?",["red apple","eat apple","apple rolls","very slowly"],0,"Red describes apple."],["Best adjective for 'The ___ pillow felt soft.'",["fluffy","jump","sing","quickly"],0,"Fluffy describes the pillow."]]},{"t":"Pronouns","learn":"Pronouns can replace nouns, such as he, she, it, they, we and you.","qs":[["Replace 'Mia' in 'Mia is reading.'",["She","It","They","We"],0,"Use she for one girl."],["Replace 'the dogs' in 'The dogs are barking.'",["They","He","It","She"],0,"They replaces more than one noun."],["Which pronoun fits? 'Tom and I are friends. ___ play together.'",["We","He","She","It"],0,"Tom and I becomes we."],["Which pronoun can replace 'the book'?",["it","they","we","he"],0,"It can replace one thing."]]},{"t":"Past Tense","learn":"Past tense tells about something that already happened. Many verbs add ed, but some change form.","qs":[["Past tense of 'jump'?",["jumped","jumps","jumping","jumpt"],0,"Many regular verbs add ed."],["Choose the past tense: 'Yesterday we ___ to the park.'",["walked","walk","walking","walks"],0,"Yesterday signals past tense."],["Past tense of 'go'?",["went","goed","goes","going"],0,"Go is irregular: went."],["Which sentence is in the past tense?",["I played soccer.","I play soccer.","I will play soccer.","I am playing soccer."],0,"Played tells us it already happened."]]},{"t":"Conjunctions","learn":"Conjunctions join ideas. Common ones include and, but, because and so.","qs":[["Choose the best word: 'I wore a coat ___ it was cold.'",["because","and","but","or"],0,"Because gives a reason."],["Choose: 'I like apples ___ bananas.'",["and","because","but","so"],0,"And joins two similar items."],["Choose: 'I was tired, ___ I finished my work.'",["but","because","and","or"],0,"But joins contrasting ideas."],["Choose: 'It rained, ___ we stayed inside.'",["so","but","because","and"],0,"So shows a result."]]}],"3":[{"t":"Subjects and Predicates","learn":"The subject tells who or what the sentence is about. The predicate tells what the subject does or is.","qs":[["Subject in 'The little fox ran away.'",["The little fox","ran away","away","ran"],0,"The subject is who or what the sentence is about."],["Predicate in 'My sister plays piano.'",["plays piano","My sister","sister","piano"],0,"The predicate tells what the subject does."],["Which is the subject? 'Three birds sat on the fence.'",["Three birds","sat","on the fence","fence"],0,"Three birds are doing the action."],["Which sentence has a clear subject and predicate?",["The dog barked loudly.","Under the table.","Very quickly.","The tall tree."],0,"It tells who/what and what happened."]]},{"t":"Verb Tenses","learn":"Present tells what happens now, past tells what happened, and future tells what will happen.","qs":[["Which is future tense?",["We will visit.","We visited.","We visit.","We are visiting now."],0,"Will often signals future tense."],["Past tense of 'see'?",["saw","seed","sees","seeing"],0,"See is irregular: saw."],["Choose present tense.",["She writes daily.","She wrote yesterday.","She will write tomorrow.","She had written."],0,"Writes describes a present habit."],["Which time word suggests past tense?",["yesterday","tomorrow","next week","soon"],0,"Yesterday is in the past."]]},{"t":"Adverbs","learn":"Adverbs often tell how, when or where an action happens.","qs":[["Adverb in 'The turtle moved slowly.'",["slowly","turtle","moved","the"],0,"Slowly tells how it moved."],["Choose an adverb to complete: 'She sang ___.'",["beautifully","song","happy","voice"],0,"Beautifully tells how she sang."],["Which word tells when? 'We arrived early.'",["early","arrived","we","none"],0,"Early tells when."],["Which sentence contains an adverb?",["The dog barked loudly.","The loud dog.","The dog is brown.","A barking dog."],0,"Loudly describes how the dog barked."]]},{"t":"Prepositions","learn":"Prepositions show position or relationship, such as in, on, under, beside and between.","qs":[["Choose: 'The shoes are ___ the bed.'",["under","quickly","soft","jump"],0,"Under shows position."],["Which word is a preposition?",["between","happy","run","yellow"],0,"Between shows a relationship in space."],["Complete: 'The bird is ___ the branch.'",["on","sing","blue","slowly"],0,"On shows location."],["Which phrase shows position?",["beside the gate","runs fast","very bright","sings softly"],0,"Beside is a preposition."]]},{"t":"Apostrophes","learn":"Apostrophes can show missing letters in contractions or possession.","qs":[["Which contraction means 'do not'?",["don't","dont","do'nt","do not'"],0,"The apostrophe replaces the missing o."],["Choose the possessive form for 'the hat belonging to Sam'.",["Sam's hat","Sams hat","Sams' hat","Sam hat"],0,"Use apostrophe + s for one owner."],["Which means 'it is'?",["it's","its","its'","it,s"],0,"It's is the contraction for it is."],["Choose the correct sentence.",["Mia's bag is red.","Mias bag is red.","Mias' bag is red.","Mia bag is red."],0,"One owner takes 's."]]}],"4":[{"t":"Independent and Dependent Clauses","learn":"An independent clause can stand alone. A dependent clause needs another clause to complete its meaning.","qs":[["Which can stand alone?",["The rain stopped.","Because the rain stopped","When we arrived","Although it was late"],0,"It expresses a complete thought."],["Which is a dependent clause?",["because I was tired","I went to bed","The dog barked","We ate dinner"],0,"Because introduces an incomplete idea needing more."],["Complete the sentence: 'Although it was raining, ___.'",["we played inside","because it was wet","when the storm","although outside"],0,"Add an independent clause."],["Which word often starts a dependent clause?",["although","table","quickly","pencil"],0,"Although is a subordinating conjunction."]]},{"t":"Complex Sentences","learn":"A complex sentence joins an independent clause with a dependent clause.","qs":[["Which is a complex sentence?",["We stayed inside because it rained.","We stayed inside.","Rain fell.","Inside was dry."],0,"It has a main clause plus a dependent reason clause."],["Choose the best connector: '___ I finished my homework, I played outside.'",["After","Table","Quickly","Blue"],0,"After can introduce a dependent time clause."],["Which sentence shows cause?",["We left because it was late.","We left and waved.","We left.","Late night."],0,"Because gives the cause."],["Choose the correct punctuation.",["When the bell rang, we lined up.","When the bell rang we lined up","When, the bell rang we lined up.","When the bell, rang we lined up."],0,"Use a comma after an opening dependent clause."]]},{"t":"Noun Groups","learn":"Noun groups add detail around a noun, for example 'the three enormous grey elephants'.","qs":[["Which is the most detailed noun group?",["the two shiny red bikes","bikes","the bikes","red"],0,"It adds number and describing words."],["Head noun in 'the old wooden bridge'?",["bridge","old","wooden","the"],0,"Bridge is the main noun."],["Add detail to 'dog'.",["the playful brown dog","runs quickly","under the table","very loudly"],0,"This is a noun group."],["Which word describes material in 'a heavy metal box'?",["metal","heavy","box","a"],0,"Metal tells what the box is made from."]]},{"t":"Direct Speech","learn":"Direct speech shows exact spoken words. Quotation marks go around the spoken words.","qs":[["Which is punctuated correctly?",["Mia said, “I am ready.”","Mia said I am ready.","Mia “said, I am ready.”","Mia said, I “am ready”."],0,"Quotation marks enclose the exact words."],["What punctuation often separates the speaker tag from speech?",["comma","hyphen","semicolon","slash"],0,"A comma often appears before the opening quotation mark."],["Which part is direct speech? 'Dad said, “Please close the gate.”'",["Please close the gate.","Dad said","the gate","Dad"],0,"Those are the exact words spoken."],["Choose the correctly punctuated question in speech.",["“Where are you going?” asked Sam.","“Where are you going.” asked Sam.","Where are you going? asked Sam.","“Where are you going”? asked Sam."],0,"The question mark belongs inside the quotation marks."]]},{"t":"Tense Consistency","learn":"Keep verb tense consistent unless the time changes.","qs":[["Choose the consistent sentence.",["We walked to the park and played.","We walked to the park and play.","We walk to the park and played.","We will walk and played."],0,"Both actions are past tense."],["Fix: 'Yesterday I eat lunch and walked home.'",["Yesterday I ate lunch and walked home.","Yesterday I eat lunch and walk home.","Yesterday I will eat lunch and walked home.","Yesterday I eating lunch and walk."],0,"Both completed actions need past tense."],["Which sentence shifts tense incorrectly?",["She opened the book and reads.","She opened the book and read.","She opens the book and reads.","She will open the book and read."],0,"Opened is past but reads is present."],["Choose the future sentence.",["We will visit and will stay overnight.","We visited and stay overnight.","We visit and stayed overnight.","We visiting and stay."],0,"Both verbs refer to future time."]]}],"5":[{"t":"Relative Clauses","learn":"A relative clause adds information about a noun and often begins with who, which, that or whose.","qs":[["Identify the relative clause: 'The cyclist who won the race trained daily.'",["who won the race","The cyclist","trained daily","the race"],0,"It adds information about the cyclist."],["Choose the best relative pronoun for a person.",["who","which","where","when"],0,"Who commonly refers to people."],["Which sentence contains a relative clause?",["The book that I borrowed was excellent.","The excellent book.","I borrowed a book.","Books are useful."],0,"That I borrowed adds information about the book."],["Choose: 'The tree, ___ was very old, fell in the storm.'",["which","who","when","where"],0,"Which refers to a thing."]]},{"t":"Modality","learn":"Modal verbs and adverbs show possibility, certainty, obligation or advice, such as might, must, should and certainly.","qs":[["Which word shows strong obligation?",["must","might","could","perhaps"],0,"Must is strong obligation."],["Which sentence shows possibility?",["It might rain later.","It will certainly rain.","You must leave.","The rain stopped."],0,"Might shows uncertainty."],["Choose the best word for advice: 'You ___ check your work.'",["should","might","cannot","never"],0,"Should often gives advice."],["Which has the strongest certainty?",["It will definitely happen.","It may happen.","It might happen.","It could happen."],0,"Definitely shows strong certainty."]]},{"t":"Active and Passive Voice","learn":"Active voice focuses on the doer. Passive voice focuses on the action or receiver.","qs":[["Which is active?",["The chef cooked the meal.","The meal was cooked by the chef.","The meal was eaten.","The meal was prepared."],0,"The subject chef performs the action."],["Which is passive?",["The window was broken by the ball.","The ball broke the window.","Sam kicked the ball.","The glass cracked."],0,"The receiver of the action comes first."],["Change to active: 'The cake was eaten by Leo.'",["Leo ate the cake.","The cake ate Leo.","Leo was eaten by the cake.","The cake is Leo."],0,"Put the doer first."],["Why might passive voice be used?",["To focus on the action or result","To remove all verbs","To make every sentence shorter","To show a question"],0,"Passive voice shifts focus away from the doer."]]},{"t":"Cohesion","learn":"Cohesion helps ideas connect through pronouns, linking words and repeated key terms.","qs":[["Best connector: 'The path was steep. ___, we continued climbing.'",["However","Because","For example","Meanwhile"],0,"However shows contrast."],["Which pronoun clearly refers to 'the students'?",["they","it","he","she"],0,"They refers back to a plural noun."],["Choose the best link: 'First we measured the water. ___, we recorded the temperature.'",["Next","Although","Instead","Because"],0,"Next shows sequence."],["Why use cohesive links?",["To connect ideas clearly","To make spelling harder","To remove paragraphs","To avoid punctuation"],0,"Cohesion helps the reader follow the text."]]},{"t":"Commas and Punctuation","learn":"Commas can separate items, introductory clauses and extra information. Other punctuation shows sentence relationships.","qs":[["Correct list punctuation?",["We packed water, hats, snacks and maps.","We packed water hats snacks and maps.","We packed, water hats, snacks and maps.","We packed water; hats; snacks and maps."],0,"Use commas between list items."],["Correct opening clause?",["After the rain stopped, we left.","After the rain stopped we left.","After, the rain stopped we left.","After the rain, stopped we left."],0,"A comma follows the opening dependent clause."],["Which sentence uses brackets for extra information?",["The koala (a marsupial) slept in the tree.","The koala, a marsupial slept in the tree.","The koala a marsupial slept.","The koala; a marsupial slept."],0,"Brackets can enclose extra information."],["Which mark can join closely related independent clauses?",["semicolon","apostrophe","quotation mark","hyphen only"],0,"A semicolon can join closely related complete clauses."]]}],"6":[{"t":"Clause Control","learn":"Writers combine independent and dependent clauses to vary emphasis, rhythm and relationships between ideas.","qs":[["Which sentence begins with a dependent clause?",["Although the road was flooded, the bus continued.","The bus continued.","The road was flooded.","The bus and car continued."],0,"Although introduces a dependent clause."],["Which has two independent clauses joined correctly?",["The sun set, and the temperature fell.","Because the sun set.","When the temperature fell.","The setting sun."],0,"Both sides can stand alone."],["Choose the best connector for contrast.",["although","because","so that","when"],0,"Although introduces contrast."],["Which is a sentence fragment?",["While the storm moved east.","The storm moved east.","The storm moved east, and rain eased.","Although it rained, we left."],0,"The dependent clause is incomplete alone."]]},{"t":"Active and Passive Voice","learn":"Choose active or passive voice depending on what you want the reader to notice.","qs":[["Passive form of 'Scientists tested the samples.'",["The samples were tested by scientists.","The samples tested scientists.","Scientists were tested by samples.","The samples are scientists."],0,"Move the receiver to subject position and use were tested."],["Which sentence best focuses on the result?",["The bridge was completed in June.","Workers completed the bridge in June.","Workers worked quickly.","June was busy."],0,"Passive voice focuses on the bridge/result."],["Which is active?",["The committee approved the plan.","The plan was approved.","The plan was approved by the committee.","Approval was given."],0,"The subject performs the action."],["Why might a report use passive voice?",["The action may matter more than the doer","It has no verbs","It is always shorter","It shows dialogue"],0,"Reports often focus on processes/results."]]},{"t":"Subject–Verb Agreement","learn":"Subjects and verbs must agree in number, even when extra words appear between them.","qs":[["Choose: 'The basket of apples ___ on the table.'",["is","are","were","be"],0,"The subject is basket, which is singular."],["Choose: 'The players on the team ___ ready.'",["are","is","was","be"],0,"Players is plural."],["Which is correct?",["Each of the books has a label.","Each of the books have a label.","Each of the books having a label.","Each books has label."],0,"Each is singular."],["Choose: 'Neither answer ___ correct.'",["is","are","be","were"],0,"Neither is treated as singular here."]]},{"t":"Semicolons, Colons and Dashes","learn":"Semicolons link related complete clauses; colons introduce explanations or lists; dashes can add emphasis.","qs":[["Best punctuation: 'I had one goal__ finish the race.'",[":",";",",","?"],0,"A colon can introduce an explanation of the goal."],["Which uses a semicolon correctly?",["The rain stopped; the match resumed.","The rain; stopped the match.","The; rain stopped.","The rain stopped; because we waited."],0,"Both sides are complete clauses."],["Which colon introduces a list correctly?",["Bring three things: water, a hat and sunscreen.","Bring: three things water, a hat and sunscreen.","Bring three: things water, a hat and sunscreen.","Bring three things water: a hat and sunscreen."],0,"The colon follows a complete lead-in."],["A dash can be used to ___.",["add emphasis or interruption","show possession","end every question","replace all commas"],0,"Dashes can set off or emphasise information."]]},{"t":"Cohesion and Paragraphing","learn":"Effective paragraphs group related ideas and use references and transitions to guide the reader.","qs":[["Best transition for a contrasting paragraph?",["In contrast","For example","First","Therefore only"],0,"In contrast signals a difference."],["When should a new paragraph often begin?",["When the focus, time, speaker or main idea changes","After every sentence","Only at the end","Whenever a comma appears"],0,"Paragraphs group related ideas."],["Which reference is clearest? 'The koalas rested in the trees. ___ slept for hours.'",["They","It","He","This one"],0,"They clearly refers to plural koalas."],["Why repeat a key term sometimes?",["To maintain topic clarity","To make the text longer","To avoid meaning","To remove cohesion"],0,"Strategic repetition helps readers follow the topic."]]}]},"spelling":{"1":[{"t":"Short a","learn":"Short a often sounds like the a in cat.","words":["cat","map","hand","black","stamp"]},{"t":"Short i","learn":"Short i often sounds like the i in fish.","words":["fish","sit","milk","ship","stick"]},{"t":"Short o","learn":"Short o often sounds like the o in hot.","words":["hot","fox","stop","frog","clock"]},{"t":"Short u","learn":"Short u often sounds like the u in sun.","words":["sun","cup","duck","jump","brush"]},{"t":"Digraphs","learn":"Two letters can work together to make one sound, such as sh, ch and th.","words":["shop","chin","this","thin","fish"]},{"t":"Common tricky words","learn":"Some common words do not follow a simple sound pattern, so we learn them by sight and use.","words":["said","they","come","some","were"]}],"2":[{"t":"Long a","learn":"Long a can be written in different ways, including a-e, ai and ay.","words":["cake","train","play","rain","name"]},{"t":"Long e","learn":"Long e can be written ee, ea and sometimes y.","words":["green","team","happy","feet","clean"]},{"t":"Long i","learn":"Long i can be written i-e, igh and y.","words":["time","light","my","night","smile"]},{"t":"Long o","learn":"Long o can be written o-e, oa and ow.","words":["home","boat","snow","road","stone"]},{"t":"Ending blends","learn":"Listen for all the sounds at the ends of words.","words":["best","milk","hand","jump","tent"]},{"t":"Tricky words","learn":"Read, say, cover, write and check tricky words.","words":["because","people","could","would","should"]}],"3":[{"t":"Silent e","learn":"A final e can change the vowel sound in a word.","words":["hope","cube","shine","brave","theme"]},{"t":"Vowel teams","learn":"Vowel teams use two letters to represent a vowel sound.","words":["beach","field","coat","cloud","boil"]},{"t":"Suffix -ing","learn":"When adding -ing, some words keep the base, some drop e and some double a consonant.","words":["running","making","jumping","swimming","writing"]},{"t":"Suffix -ed","learn":"Past-tense -ed can sound different, but the spelling stays -ed.","words":["looked","played","wanted","helped","started"]},{"t":"Prefixes","learn":"Prefixes such as un-, re- and dis- are added to the beginning of base words.","words":["unhappy","replay","dislike","rewrite","unsafe"]},{"t":"High-frequency words","learn":"Practise common words that appear often in reading and writing.","words":["different","important","another","through","thought"]}],"4":[{"t":"Prefixes","learn":"Keep the base word visible when adding many prefixes.","words":["preview","misplace","submarine","inactive","nonfiction"]},{"t":"Suffixes","learn":"Watch for spelling changes when adding suffixes.","words":["careful","happiness","movement","famous","noisy"]},{"t":"Homophones","learn":"Homophones sound alike but have different spellings and meanings.","words":["their","there","they're","weather","whether"]},{"t":"Double consonants","learn":"Some words double the final consonant before adding a suffix.","words":["beginning","preferred","running","admitted","forgotten"]},{"t":"Greek and Latin parts","learn":"Word parts can help you spell and understand longer words.","words":["telephone","autograph","transport","aquatic","geography"]},{"t":"Academic words","learn":"Break longer words into syllables and meaningful parts.","words":["environment","information","experiment","community","temperature"]}],"5":[{"t":"Prefixes and roots","learn":"Use meaningful word parts to spell unfamiliar words.","words":["interact","predict","transport","subdivide","misunderstand"]},{"t":"Suffix changes","learn":"Watch for y-to-i changes, dropped e and doubled consonants.","words":["happily","reliable","beginning","argument","comfortable"]},{"t":"Homophones and near-homophones","learn":"Use sentence meaning to choose the correct spelling.","words":["principal","principle","stationary","stationery","affect"]},{"t":"Unstressed syllables","learn":"Some vowels are hard to hear clearly, so use syllables and word families.","words":["separate","familiar","definite","category","original"]},{"t":"Greek and Latin roots","learn":"Roots such as bio, geo, spect and port can guide spelling and meaning.","words":["biology","geography","inspect","portable","spectator"]},{"t":"Academic vocabulary","learn":"Say longer words slowly, identify syllables and check familiar word parts.","words":["necessary","opportunity","environment","government","temperature"]}],"6":[{"t":"Morphology","learn":"Use base words, prefixes and suffixes to keep spelling connected to meaning.","words":["significant","significance","predictable","prediction","responsibility"]},{"t":"Complex suffixes","learn":"Check how suffixes change the spelling of the base word.","words":["competition","explanation","persuasive","curiosity","reversible"]},{"t":"Confusable words","learn":"Words that sound or look similar must be chosen by meaning.","words":["complement","compliment","accept","except","advice"]},{"t":"Loanwords","learn":"English includes words borrowed from many languages, so some spellings are less predictable.","words":["restaurant","ballet","mosquito","tsunami","yacht"]},{"t":"Academic roots","learn":"Recognising roots helps with both spelling and vocabulary.","words":["chronology","microscope","democracy","geothermal","aeronautics"]},{"t":"Advanced common words","learn":"Use syllables, morphology and proofreading for high-use complex words.","words":["accommodate","conscience","embarrass","privilege","recommend"]}]},"writing":{"1":[{"t":"Recount","title":"My Fun Day","prompt":"Write 3–5 sentences about a fun day you remember. Tell what happened first, next and last.","plan":["Where were you?","What happened?","How did you feel?"],"check":["Capital letter at the start","Full stop at the end","Spaces between words","At least one describing word"],"goal":"Aim for 30–50 words."},{"t":"Description","title":"My Favourite Animal","prompt":"Describe an animal you like. Tell what it looks like, what it can do and why you like it.","plan":["What does it look like?","What can it do?","Why do you like it?"],"check":["Use describing words","Write complete sentences","Use a capital letter for each sentence","Check full stops"],"goal":"Aim for 30–50 words."},{"t":"Narrative","title":"The Lost Toy","prompt":"Imagine a toy goes missing. Write what happens and how it is found.","plan":["What toy is lost?","Where do you look?","How is it found?"],"check":["Beginning, middle and end","Use who and where","Use action words","Check capitals and full stops"],"goal":"Aim for 40–60 words."},{"t":"Information","title":"How to Look After a Pet","prompt":"Write simple instructions for looking after a pet.","plan":["Food","Water/home","Kind care"],"check":["Use order words such as first/next","Write clear steps","Use action verbs","Check punctuation"],"goal":"Aim for 40–60 words."}],"2":[{"t":"Recount","title":"A Day at the Park","prompt":"Write a short recount of a visit to a park or playground. Use time words to show order.","plan":["When and where?","What did you do?","What happened at the end?"],"check":["Use first/then/after/last","Past tense","At least one adjective","Correct sentence punctuation"],"goal":"Aim for 60–90 words."},{"t":"Narrative","title":"The Door That Would Not Open","prompt":"Write a story about a door that suddenly will not open. Create a problem and a solution.","plan":["Who is there?","Why is the door important?","How is the problem solved?"],"check":["Clear beginning, problem and ending","Use action verbs","Add feelings","Check capitals, commas and full stops"],"goal":"Aim for 70–100 words."},{"t":"Persuasive","title":"The Best Class Pet","prompt":"Choose an animal that would make the best class pet. Give at least two reasons.","plan":["Your opinion","Reason 1","Reason 2"],"check":["State your opinion","Use because","Give two reasons","Finish with a strong ending"],"goal":"Aim for 70–100 words."},{"t":"Information","title":"How Seeds Grow","prompt":"Explain the basic things a seed needs to begin growing.","plan":["What it needs","What happens first","What happens next"],"check":["Use factual language","Use sequence words","Keep ideas in order","Check spelling"],"goal":"Aim for 70–100 words."}],"3":[{"t":"Narrative","title":"The Map in the Library","prompt":"Write a story about finding an old map hidden inside a library book.","plan":["Who finds it?","Where does the map lead?","What happens at the end?"],"check":["Strong opening","Problem or mystery","Paragraphs for new events","Interesting verbs and adjectives"],"goal":"Aim for 120–160 words."},{"t":"Persuasive","title":"Longer Lunchtime?","prompt":"Should students have a longer lunchtime? Give your position and support it with reasons and examples.","plan":["Position","Reason + example","Second reason + example"],"check":["Clear opinion","Use linking words","Explain each reason","Concluding sentence"],"goal":"Aim for 120–160 words."},{"t":"Information","title":"An Amazing Animal","prompt":"Write an information report about an animal. Include habitat, diet, body features and one interesting fact.","plan":["Classification/habitat","Diet/features","Interesting fact"],"check":["Use headings if helpful","Write factual sentences","Group related facts","Check scientific words"],"goal":"Aim for 120–170 words."},{"t":"Recount","title":"A Challenge I Solved","prompt":"Describe a time you solved a difficult problem or learned something new.","plan":["What was difficult?","What did you try?","What did you learn?"],"check":["Past tense","Events in order","Thoughts and feelings","Reflect at the end"],"goal":"Aim for 120–160 words."}],"4":[{"t":"Narrative","title":"The Message from Tomorrow","prompt":"A student receives a short message from themselves one day in the future. Write what the message says and what happens next.","plan":["Opening/setting","Problem or warning","Resolution"],"check":["Engaging opening","Paragraphs","Dialogue punctuated correctly","Show, don't only tell"],"goal":"Aim for 180–240 words."},{"t":"Persuasive","title":"Should School Gardens Be Compulsory?","prompt":"Argue whether every primary school should have a garden used for learning.","plan":["Position","Strongest reason + evidence","Second reason + example"],"check":["Clear thesis","Persuasive language","Evidence/examples","Strong conclusion"],"goal":"Aim for 180–240 words."},{"t":"Explanation","title":"How the Water Cycle Works","prompt":"Explain evaporation, condensation and precipitation in a logical sequence.","plan":["Evaporation","Condensation","Precipitation/collection"],"check":["Technical vocabulary","Cause-and-effect links","Present tense","Logical sequence"],"goal":"Aim for 170–230 words."},{"t":"Description","title":"A Place After a Storm","prompt":"Describe a familiar place just after a powerful storm has passed.","plan":["What can you see?","What can you hear/smell?","How does the place feel?"],"check":["Sensory details","Precise verbs","Varied sentence openings","Strong final image"],"goal":"Aim for 180–230 words."}],"5":[{"t":"Narrative","title":"The Last Light in the Building","prompt":"Everyone has left a large building except one student who notices a light still glowing on the top floor. Write the story.","plan":["Set the scene","Build tension/problem","Resolve with meaning"],"check":["Control pace","Paragraph deliberately","Use dialogue purposefully","Create a satisfying ending"],"goal":"Aim for 250–350 words."},{"t":"Persuasive","title":"Should Devices Be Limited at School?","prompt":"Write a balanced persuasive response about whether personal devices should be limited during the school day.","plan":["Position and context","Arguments + evidence","Address another viewpoint"],"check":["Clear contention","Logical paragraphs","Examples/evidence","Consider a counterargument"],"goal":"Aim for 250–350 words."},{"t":"Information","title":"How an Ecosystem Stays Balanced","prompt":"Explain how producers, consumers, decomposers and environmental conditions interact in an ecosystem.","plan":["Key parts","Connections","What happens when conditions change?"],"check":["Accurate topic vocabulary","Cause/effect","Cohesive links","Clear paragraph structure"],"goal":"Aim for 230–320 words."},{"t":"Recount","title":"When the Plan Changed","prompt":"Write a reflective recount about a time when a plan had to change unexpectedly.","plan":["Original plan","Unexpected change","Response and reflection"],"check":["Specific details","Past tense control","Thoughts and reactions","Reflective conclusion"],"goal":"Aim for 230–320 words."}],"6":[{"t":"Narrative","title":"The City That Forgot the Night","prompt":"Write a sophisticated short narrative about a city where darkness suddenly stops arriving.","plan":["Establish normal life","Reveal consequences","Resolve or leave a purposeful ending"],"check":["Deliberate viewpoint","Controlled pacing","Precise imagery","Cohesive paragraphs"],"goal":"Aim for 350–500 words."},{"t":"Persuasive","title":"Should Homework Have a Weekly Limit?","prompt":"Argue a position on whether primary students should have a maximum weekly homework time. Address at least one counterargument.","plan":["Thesis","Evidence-based arguments","Counterargument + response"],"check":["Strong contention","Logical evidence","Modality used deliberately","Counterargument addressed"],"goal":"Aim for 350–500 words."},{"t":"Discussion","title":"Is Competition Good for Learning?","prompt":"Discuss both benefits and drawbacks of competition in learning, then give a reasoned conclusion.","plan":["Benefits","Drawbacks","Judgement"],"check":["Balanced viewpoints","Formal linking language","Examples/evidence","Qualified conclusion"],"goal":"Aim for 350–500 words."},{"t":"Explanation","title":"Why Reliable Evidence Matters","prompt":"Explain why fair tests, controlled variables, repeat measurements and careful conclusions matter in scientific investigations.","plan":["Fair testing","Reliable measurement","Conclusions and limitations"],"check":["Technical vocabulary","Cause/effect","Examples","Clear concluding statement"],"goal":"Aim for 300–450 words."}]}};
